1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,560
Hi everyone, it's Seryoga again,

2
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:06,410
and today's a big one — we're
driving to St. Petersburg.

3
00:00:07,210 --> 00:00:10,050
Not on the Sapsan like normal people,

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,040
but by car, through Novgorod and Valdai.

5
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:16,040
With me, as always, is Alyona —

6
00:00:16,190 --> 00:00:18,460
Alyona, say hi. — Hi!

7
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:21,320
And in the back seat, Timur,

8
00:00:21,470 --> 00:00:25,460
who's already asleep, and we
haven't even left Moscow yet.

9
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:27,890
Timur, get up! — I'm not
asleep, I'm thinking.

10
00:00:28,690 --> 00:00:31,390
Yeah, sure he is. Okay, here's the plan:

11
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:34,530
today we get to Valdai
and spend the night there,

12
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:38,160
and tomorrow morning it's on to St.
Petersburg, to my sister Ksyusha.

13
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:41,310
Ksyusha, if you're watching this —

14
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:45,010
we're bringing you
Mom's pies, don't worry.

15
00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:48,660
Well, unless Timur eats them on the way.

16
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:51,090
— I can hear you, you know. — Good.

17
00:00:51,890 --> 00:00:54,170
Okay, we're getting on the M-11 highway,

18
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:56,870
it's a toll road, sure, but it's fast.

19
00:00:57,670 --> 00:01:00,940
It's about seven hundred
kilometers to St. Petersburg,

20
00:01:01,090 --> 00:01:04,440
and the tolls come to around
three thousand rubles.

21
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:07,580
Alyona says it's daylight robbery,

22
00:01:07,730 --> 00:01:10,790
but on the old road we'd
be driving till night.

23
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:13,790
First stop — a gas station.

24
00:01:14,590 --> 00:01:19,140
Timur woke up the second
he smelled hot dogs.

25
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:21,930
Now that's what I call instinct.

26
00:01:22,730 --> 00:01:26,140
— Seryoga, want anything? — Get me a raf,

27
00:01:26,290 --> 00:01:29,140
not with coconut milk, just regular.

28
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:32,210
Alyona, as always, gets tea,

29
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:36,840
because gas station coffee
is, quote, "a crime."

30
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:39,200
Okay, moving on.

31
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,920
By the way, a lot of you
asked in the comments

32
00:01:43,070 --> 00:01:45,980
why we decided to drive at all.

33
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:49,050
Honestly, we wanted some action.

34
00:01:49,850 --> 00:01:53,410
Two months stuck at home
working remotely is brutal,

35
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:56,330
I've started talking to my ficus.

36
00:01:57,130 --> 00:01:59,450
— He really talks to
it. — Don't rat me out!

37
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:02,090
So anyway, we're driving along,

38
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:06,440
and Timur spots a sign:
"Veliky Novgorod, 40 km,"

39
00:02:06,590 --> 00:02:09,430
and says: let's stop by the Kremlin,

40
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:12,210
I haven't been there since
I was a kid, and besides,

41
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:16,060
it's the oldest city, you
can't just drive past it.

42
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:18,350
So we turned off.

43
00:02:19,150 --> 00:02:22,490
The Novgorod Kremlin, guys, is amazing.

44
00:02:23,290 --> 00:02:25,990
Red walls, towers, the Volkhov flowing by,

45
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:29,340
and almost nobody around,
because it's a weekday.

46
00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:33,270
Alyona went straight off to
photograph St. Sophia Cathedral,

47
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:37,620
while Timur and I got hooked on
the Millennium of Russia monument.

48
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:41,050
It has over a hundred figures, by the way,

49
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:43,330
and Timur decided to find every one.

50
00:02:44,130 --> 00:02:46,260
He found about ten and gave up.

51
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:49,900
— Peter the Great was
there, I definitely saw him.

52
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:53,150
— Timur, there are like
three Peters. — See?

53
00:02:53,950 --> 00:02:56,220
We had lunch at a local canteen,

54
00:02:56,370 --> 00:03:00,500
borscht, cutlets, compote — five
hundred rubles for the three of us.

55
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:03,930
In Moscow, for that money you'd get,

56
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:06,920
well, a glass of water
and some respect, tops.

57
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:10,500
Moving on, it's another
two hours to Valdai.

58
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:14,210
And this is where it gets interesting.

59
00:03:15,010 --> 00:03:17,920
The GPS decided we wanted a shortcut

60
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:21,420
and took us down some dirt
road through the woods.

61
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:24,700
Alyona says: Seryozha, are you sure?

62
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:28,630
I say: of course, the GPS isn't stupid.

63
00:03:29,430 --> 00:03:31,560
The GPS turned out to be stupid.

64
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:36,200
Five kilometers later the
road turned into a swamp,

65
00:03:36,350 --> 00:03:38,200
and we got well and truly stuck.

66
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:40,810
— Well, fuck. — Timur, we're filming.

67
00:03:41,610 --> 00:03:44,240
— I know, let people see the truth.

68
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:47,670
We got out and pushed,
mud up to our knees,

69
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:49,590
Alyona's at the wheel flooring it,

70
00:03:49,740 --> 00:03:52,660
and all that mud flies straight at Timur.

71
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:56,440
I laughed for five minutes
straight, couldn't stop.

72
00:03:57,240 --> 00:03:59,230
Long story short, we couldn't
get it out ourselves.

73
00:04:00,030 --> 00:04:04,230
About forty minutes later a
local guy drove by in a Niva,

74
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:05,380
Uncle Tolya,

75
00:04:05,530 --> 00:04:10,440
who apparently pulls tourists
out of here every week.

76
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:13,870
He literally said: "The GPS again?

77
00:04:14,670 --> 00:04:17,660
Every week somebody gets stuck."

78
00:04:18,460 --> 00:04:21,440
He hooked up a tow rope,
one pull, and we were free.

79
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:24,510
He wouldn't take any money, just said

80
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:28,150
that next time we should
take the highway like

81
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:32,210
normal people. Thank you,
Uncle Tolya, you're a legend.

82
00:04:33,010 --> 00:04:35,640
We got to Valdai after dark

83
00:04:35,790 --> 00:04:37,920
and rented a cabin right on the lake.

84
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:40,920
Timur went to wash off in the shower

85
00:04:41,070 --> 00:04:43,840
and came out an hour later saying:

86
00:04:43,990 --> 00:04:45,550
"I've been born again."

87
00:04:46,350 --> 00:04:48,410
In the evening we sat on the porch,

88
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:51,260
grilled sausages, looked at the stars.

89
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:55,610
Alyona said it was the best
day of the whole summer,

90
00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:59,390
despite the swamp. Or maybe because of it.

91
00:05:00,190 --> 00:05:01,890
We got up at six in the morning,

92
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:05,960
because Timur decided he
wanted to watch the sunrise.

93
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:09,600
He watched it. Took a
photo. Went back to bed.

94
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,260
— It was a ritual. — It was cringe, Timur.

95
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:14,480
Off to St. Petersburg,

96
00:05:14,630 --> 00:05:17,470
Ksyusha texts every half
hour: "Where are you?"

97
00:05:18,270 --> 00:05:20,040
Ksyusha, we're coming, honest.

98
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:23,110
The highway is a dream,

99
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:24,530
smooth, empty,

100
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:27,810
and four hours later we
were pulling into the city.

101
00:05:28,610 --> 00:05:31,880
The first thing Timur
said when we got there:

102
00:05:32,030 --> 00:05:34,090
"So is it a 'porebrik' here, or a curb?"

103
00:05:34,890 --> 00:05:36,590
Ksyusha lives in Kupchino

104
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:39,300
and met us right outside her building.

105
00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:42,230
Hugs, tears, pies —

106
00:05:42,380 --> 00:05:45,510
Timur only ate two, by the way, I counted.

107
00:05:46,310 --> 00:05:47,930
Ksyusha said right away:

108
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:50,430
okay, first we're getting shawarma,

109
00:05:50,580 --> 00:05:53,630
because this is St. Petersburg
and that's how it's done.

110
00:05:54,430 --> 00:05:56,280
The shawarma was fire, by the way.

111
00:05:57,080 --> 00:06:01,490
No offense, Muscovites, but
yours doesn't even come close.

112
00:06:02,290 --> 00:06:04,630
Then we went to Vasilyevsky Island,

113
00:06:04,780 --> 00:06:07,560
walked along the Strelka,
looked at the Neva.

114
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:10,480
The weather, of course,
was pure St. Petersburg:

115
00:06:10,630 --> 00:06:13,830
within an hour we had sun,
a downpour and sun again.

116
00:06:14,630 --> 00:06:17,260
Alyona got soaked through and said

117
00:06:17,410 --> 00:06:21,260
she now gets why everyone
here is such a philosopher.

118
00:06:22,060 --> 00:06:25,540
In the evening Ksyusha took us
to a bar on Rubinstein Street,

119
00:06:25,690 --> 00:06:28,680
where her friend Zhenya
works as a bartender.

120
00:06:29,480 --> 00:06:32,960
Zhenya made us some
sea buckthorn cocktail,

121
00:06:33,110 --> 00:06:36,530
and honestly, I still
don't know what was in it,

122
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:38,020
but it was fucking great.

123
00:06:38,820 --> 00:06:42,380
Timur, naturally, tried
to flirt with Zhenya,

124
00:06:42,530 --> 00:06:45,800
and then it turned out
Zhenya is short for Yevgeny,

125
00:06:45,950 --> 00:06:49,220
and he has a beard. Timur
just didn't look closely.

126
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:52,380
— It was dark! — Timur,
he was a meter away.

127
00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:55,530
Zhenya took it with a
sense of humor, by the way,

128
00:06:55,680 --> 00:06:58,880
and poured Timur another one on the house.

129
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:02,520
The next day we went to Peterhof.

130
00:07:03,320 --> 00:07:05,880
Fountains, gold, crowds of tourists —

131
00:07:06,030 --> 00:07:07,800
just like the postcards.

132
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:11,510
Ksyusha marched us around
the park for four hours,

133
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:14,150
and by the end Timur
couldn't walk anymore,

134
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:17,930
sat down on a bench and said
he'd just live there now.

135
00:07:18,730 --> 00:07:22,000
Tickets are fifteen hundred
rubles a person, by the way,

136
00:07:22,150 --> 00:07:24,420
but it's worth it, no joke.

137
00:07:25,220 --> 00:07:27,920
We took the Meteor back to the city,

138
00:07:28,070 --> 00:07:31,560
it's a high-speed hydrofoil
across the Gulf of Finland.

139
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:34,480
Alyona got seasick, so did Timur,

140
00:07:34,630 --> 00:07:37,330
and I sat there filming it all for you,

141
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:39,540
because content comes first.

142
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:43,180
We spent the last evening
at Ksyusha's place,

143
00:07:43,330 --> 00:07:44,890
eating Mom's pies,

144
00:07:45,040 --> 00:07:48,670
the very ones Timur somehow
didn't eat on the way.

145
00:07:49,470 --> 00:07:53,030
Ksyusha told us she
wants to move to Moscow,

146
00:07:53,180 --> 00:07:55,020
and I'm like: are you crazy?

147
00:07:55,820 --> 00:07:58,310
You've got shawarma
here, porebriks, Zhenya!

148
00:07:59,110 --> 00:08:02,380
"Zhenya's a good point," Ksyusha agreed.

149
00:08:03,180 --> 00:08:05,380
In the morning we headed back to Moscow.

150
00:08:06,180 --> 00:08:08,590
This time strictly by the highway,

151
00:08:08,740 --> 00:08:10,870
Uncle Tolya would be proud.

152
00:08:11,670 --> 00:08:13,800
The drive took eight hours,

153
00:08:13,950 --> 00:08:15,790
Timur slept for seven of them.

154
00:08:16,590 --> 00:08:21,010
All in all, guys, this was the
best trip we've had in ages.

155
00:08:21,810 --> 00:08:26,290
If you want us to do a trip like
this to Kazan or Nizhny Novgorod,

156
00:08:26,440 --> 00:08:28,210
let us know in the comments.

157
00:08:29,010 --> 00:08:31,140
Alyona, Timur, say bye.

158
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:35,500
— Bye! — Subscribe, or
Seryoga will be sad.

159
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:37,800
— I won't be sad. — He will.

160
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:40,580
That's it, bye everyone, see you!
